+5. The last candidate's error reaches the agent, with one exception for a group (#1418, `patience.go`), which a plain provider shares once its same-one retries are spent (below). When every member has failed in a way that passes (`busy`: 408, 500, 502, 503, 504 or 529 that isn't a proxy, a quota, response protection, or an upstream that answered a stream request with a whole reply, which fails the same however often it is asked (`didNotStream`; the same-one retries of `passing` leave it alone too, `TestStreamlessMemberIsAskedOnce`), or a 429 that is a short rate limit, not a plan or credit), and nothing of the reply has been sent, the members that failed so are asked again in turn after a pause (`replan`). The pause is `retryPause` doubled each round up to `replanLongest()` (30 times `retryPause`, 30s), and never shorter than the soonest Retry-After; a member whose Retry-After ends past the pause is asked after the others. This goes on while the group's `Patience` lasts. A member of a group in the group uses the innermost that sets `Patience` (`innerPatience`, #2410), as FirstToken (#2246) and Sink (#2255) do; unset falls back to the group asked for, and an inner `PatienceOff` skips replan for that member (`TestNestedGroupPatienceThroughOuter`, `TestNestedGroupPatienceInnerOff`, `TestNestedGroupPatienceFallsBackToOuter`). `replanRounds` (12) is only a backstop that never comes first: the pauses alone add up to `PatienceLongest` in 11 rounds (with 6, a group set to 240s gave up after 90s of pauses, real-jacket on #1418; `TestPatienceIsUsedUp`). The budget is the group's `Patience`, counted from the first time every member had failed, when the agent would have had the error (`waitFrom`; `Group.Waits`: 0 is `PatienceDefault`, 60s; -1, `PatienceOff`, turns it off; at most `PatienceLongest`, 240s). A pause that would end past the budget isn't taken, and the agent gets the error; a member the vendor said is back within a second past its budget (read in whole seconds) is asked as the budget ends (`TestReporterResetFitsTheDefaultPatience`). While another member is busy too, the last one isn't first tried again alone (`passing`): the members take turns, and once the budget has begun, the same-one retries aren't taken either. While the budget lasts, the last candidate's failure is held (`newHoldWriter`), and a streaming agent is kept alive through the pause with SSE comments (`pauseAlive`). The try is traced with `Again` (the pause), `Replan` (how many are asked again) and `Patience` (the budget in seconds). The group editor's "Every member busy" row sets it. A plain provider (a `provider/model` request, its accounts and fallbacks the candidates) first takes the same-one retries `passing` gives the last one left: `lastRetries` for a busy vendor, `rateRetries` for a rate limit, each pause `retryPause<<n` or a Retry-After of at most `longestPause` (8s). Past those, or for a Retry-After longer than `longestPause`, it is replanned the same way, its accounts taking turns as members do, for the provider's own `Patience` (`Provider.Patience`, the same values as a group's, set with `magpie provider set <id> patience=2m|off|default`; the provider editor shows no row for it and its save keeps it, `TestProviderSaveKeepsPatience`), unset `singlePatience` (`PatienceDefault`, 60s), counted from when the agent would have had the error, so it works with no setting and the user's value wins either way (`TestSingleProviderOwnPatienceWins`; ChatGPT's reset of a large request, `codexReset`, is left out: Codex is told it at its 503 with what may help, as `codexBusy` leaves it). A provider's own Patience is for a request to it alone: a group it is in, set or unset, keeps the group's (zoecats on Discord: a local relay's short 429 went straight to Claude Code once the few retries were spent). So a 429 whose Retry-After is 20s is waited out, the stream kept alive past `keepHeldAfter`; one of an hour, a quota, credit or plan limit, a 401 or 403, a refusal, response protection's 502 and `didNotStream` are not `busy` or don't fit the budget, and reach the agent at once as the vendor said them. Its tries are traced with `Again` alone, no `Replan` or `Patience`, so the Routing page tells it as the last one left asked again rather than as a group's members (`TestSingleProviderWaitsOutAShortRateLimit`, `TestSingleProviderPassesALongLimitThrough`, `TestSingleProviderAgentGoneInTheWait` in [`single_busy_test.go`](../../internal/gateway/single_busy_test.go)). Codex's own one-account relay (`codexUpstreamOn`, below) doesn't join it: its request is relayed unchanged outside the candidate loop, and the backend's 429 is always the plan's usage limit (`codexBusy` never retries one), so the short rate limit this waits out doesn't arise there; its overloads keep their `lastRetries` and then the subscription's fallbacks. An agent that goes away in either pause (the same-one retry's or a group's replan) ends the request there with 499: nobody is asked again, and the try whose pause it was is marked `Gone` (#1894, heng91: a relay down while its proxy was off, the agent's window closed). The Routing page says the agent canceled the request before the retry and lists it as canceled, rather than ending its story on "tried again in 2s", a retry that never came (`TestAgentGoneInTheRetryPause`, `routing-agent-gone.test.cjs`). A try that answered is recorded (`served`, `answered`) for routing, affinity and usage. A try is added to the route's `Tries` only as it is asked, so a later try is the record that the gateway went on from this one, and the Routing page tells what came of a try from that, never from where it stood in the order (#2281, xishuchkui-code: an Antigravity 502 empty reply that went on to the next account was told as "an error another account wouldn't fix, so Claude Code gets it"). The one case the record can't show is the last try left out of its allowance after an earlier failure that wasn't one, where the agent is told the earlie